{"authored" [{"question" "e-5452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a dilation. Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/bd6574ee05b60919de656a889f8532e5a512b381eae63f7a65f251a7afe72738.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/be73c2a933a6fb99eaa389774a618848bdadd9cafdf0fcd2f6af126b49b87707.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/example-5452.json" "file_sha256" "b32d811585eb55e66592e2b292ad2b8d56cbab7178cc5a0736aa7105deac9b4b" "basis" 1764 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can write $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nRemember that the matrix representing a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/4866597a50fbdb530e9275c77f2ca926c932c7cc334635bd05191aaddd510d75.png)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/962b9458714f9b7f6b17fe2259e140dfabba443d8ecb5b9e2328172ab3f8a1a2.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/example-5452.json" "file_sha256" "b32d811585eb55e66592e2b292ad2b8d56cbab7178cc5a0736aa7105deac9b4b" "basis" 1764 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What linear transformation is represented by the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/6191f617d2c2e89e07c1cc349a5926874f11ea78d046185ce990e566225a7c79.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/example-5451.json" "file_sha256" "f58babbf7cbd462bb0de0238999a5c78f738f9ac4894ac7e752408616a28cce4" "basis" 1764 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as depicted below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents the reflection across the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/example-5451.json" "file_sha256" "f58babbf7cbd462bb0de0238999a5c78f738f9ac4894ac7e752408616a28cce4" "basis" 1764 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-328218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A  linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation. Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}56 \\\\ -98\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix $T$ of $T?$\n\n| $T=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060608/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4beebc66cf2c0c1a07c229c5cdbf634c22adbd4bbafe00d20176cc356e5d812"} {"question" "q-328218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}56 \\\\ -98\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=14v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=14.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060608/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4beebc66cf2c0c1a07c229c5cdbf634c22adbd4bbafe00d20176cc356e5d812"} {"question" "q-328218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file"